---
import "../styles/figures.css";
import "../styles/course.css";
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    You are a finite system in a world that is not. Everything you know about it
    arrived through channels that discard almost all of it, and was compressed
    again into a model small enough to act on. That compression is what your
    intelligence <em>is</em>. It is also why there are things you cannot notice
    you are missing.
  </p>

  <h2>What the course argues</h2>
  <p>
    Intelligence is not a quantity that some animals have more of. It is a set
    of trades, made under a hard constraint, and every one of them buys a
    capability by giving something up. We start with a bacterium that holds
    nothing at all and work upward, and by the end you are asked to run the
    account on yourself: to name one limit of your own judgement that has the
    same <em>shape</em> as one you documented in another organism, and to say
    what would show you had got it wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    The last weeks are the point of the earlier ones. Once you can say what a
    desert ant cannot detect about its own navigation, the same question about
    your own judgement stops being rhetorical — and becomes much harder, because
    you are asking it from inside the thing you are asking about.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will read primary literature every week, most of it outside whatever
    discipline you came from. Four fields are in play and none of them is
    optional: evolutionary biology says when a capacity appeared and how often,
    neuroscience says what implements it, psychology says how it was measured,
    and philosophy asks whether the concept survives being pressed on. A claim
    supported by only one of them is incomplete, and you will be asked to say so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Much of the field's best-known findings have not replicated. You will grade
    evidence rather than accumulate it, and you will do this on claims that are
    still being argued about, with no answer key. In week 2 you will write down
    what you currently think intelligence is, before you know anything, and in
    week 11 you will revise it and account for what moved.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year students from any of biology, neuroscience, psychology,
    philosophy or computer science. You need no background in the other three,
    but you do need to be willing to read outside your own — most weeks, the
    evidence that settles a question comes from a field you have not studied.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no prerequisite and no exam. If you want a semester of established
    results, this is the wrong course; roughly a third of what we cover is
    contested, and the disagreements are the content rather than an
    inconvenience.
  </p>

  <h2>About the title</h2>
  <p>
    The title is a provocation, and the course disagrees with half of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dumb</strong> implies a deficiency: a mind that is a broken version
    of a better one. Nothing here supports that. Every system in the course,
    from a bacterium to you, buys a capability by giving something up, and what
    it gave up is the price of what it bought rather than a fault in it. Week 11
    is not a list of the ways you reason badly — it is largely about how much of
    the famous literature making that claim failed to replicate.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the title gets right is narrower and harder to shake off. A compression
    cannot hold what it discarded, so it cannot report what it discarded either.
    You have no inventory of your own omissions. That is a structural
    consequence of being finite, not an insult, and it applies to the three of
    us teaching this as much as to anyone enrolled in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    We grade every claim on this site, so it is fair to grade our own.{" "}
    <strong>The title is <abbr class="grade grade-s" title="speculative">[S]</abbr></strong>:
    a reasoned extension, stated provocatively, and not a finding. Hold us to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why there is no week on artificial intelligence</h2>
  <p>
    The most common question about the course, and the absence is deliberate
    rather than an oversight. Every system in these twelve weeks had its
    compression chosen
    by selection rather than by a designer, and weeks 8 to 10 work as a control
    group only because those lineages solved the same problems independently of
    each other and of us. An artificial system is not an
    independent lineage; it was built by one of the systems under study, out of
    data produced by that system, which makes it evidence about us rather than
    evidence alongside us.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hence the position: artificial systems are a case the model should be able
    to <em>predict</em>, not a week of content. If the account built here is any
    good, you should be able to take it to a system it was never derived from
    and say what that system cannot represent and cannot detect about itself,
    before looking. The optional tenth section of the{" "}
    <a href={withBase("/assessments/cognitive-profile/")}>cognitive profile</a>{" "}
    is where you may do exactly that, labelled{" "}
    <abbr class="grade grade-s" title="speculative">[S]</abbr>, and it is where
    some of the best work on the course turns up.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Schedule" href="/schedule/">
      <p>The whole semester on one page: lectures, seminars, and what is due when.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, what happens in each, and the papers behind them.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Four pieces of work, what each is for, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Evidence grades" href="/evidence/">
      <p>The <abbr class="grade grade-e" title="established">[E]</abbr>, <abbr class="grade grade-c" title="contested">[C]</abbr> and <abbr class="grade grade-s" title="speculative">[S]</abbr> notation every claim on this site carries.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The lecture programme, with slides for all twelve weeks.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches this and what to ask each of them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--at-border);
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    margin-block-end: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
